TODO
====

- sig-event-loop-integration:
  The sig module has no self-pipe or signalfd support. A handler installed
  with sig_catch or sig_action has to do its work from signal handler
  context or hand off some other way. There is no built-in way to turn a
  caught signal into something that a select, poll or epoll loop can wait
  on.

  This was noticed while making the module more generally reusable, see
  doc/sig.md. It is usually the actual reason a server oriented program
  reaches for a signal library.

- exec-intercept-system-signal-semantics:
  The system and popen overrides in exec-intercept.c are used when the map
  redirects sh or /bin/sh to another shell. They reimplement the fork, exec
  and wait sequence directly instead of calling the libc functions. That
  skips the signal handling POSIX requires around the child: SIGINT and
  SIGQUIT ignored and SIGCHLD blocked in the parent for the duration of
  the call.

  This is fine for the compatibility testing the tool targets. A caller
  that relies on that signal behavior around system or popen would see a
  difference when the shell is redirected.

- genmakefile-create-module-gcc-only:
  The option --create-module builds loadable modules such as QuickJS native
  modules. It only sets commands.link_module in the gcc and clang branch of
  set_compiler_type in genmakefile.c, next to commands.link. Every other
  compiler branch leaves it empty. Using --create-module with -t msvc
  therefore generates a module rule with an empty recipe instead of
  failing loudly.

  Only gcc style toolchains make sense as module builders in practice. The
  silent failure for the rest deserves a guard or warning, like the one
  that already exists for sdcc with --create-libs, instead of a quiet
  Makefile rule that does nothing.

- genmakefile-cross-compile-flag-disabled:
  The block "if(cygming && 0)" in the trailer of set_compiler_type
  (genmakefile.c, right after the chain of compiler branches) would set
  CROSS_COMPILE from tools.toolchain, for example x86_64-w64-mingw32-. It
  is permanently disabled by the "&& 0".

  This does not block cross compiling, because the -c or --cross PREFIX
  option does the same job when given explicitly. It does mean that -t
  mingw64 alone silently produces a plain CC = gcc instead of a real cross
  compiler.

  Two ways out:
    wire up the disabled block so the prefix comes from tools.toolchain
    document in --help that -c is required with -t mingw64, msys or cygwin

- input-c-prefix-detection-more-coverage:
  The decoration prefix detection in src/genmakefile/input.c lets the
  lines of an --infile carry an arbitrary prefix in front of the real
  command, such as shell trace markers or logging shim banners. The shape
  of the decoration is detected from the file itself instead of being
  hardcoded. It was committed after smoke tests with four samples of one
  rule each: undecorated, a timestamp and pid style prefix, a set -x style
  "+ " prefix, and one that deliberately ends in a lone backslash without a
  newline. All four produced identical rules and none hung under a 2GB
  ulimit -v.

  Still worth covering is messier real world decoration: log entries that
  wrap over several lines, decoration containing characters that look like
  flags or paths, and a file without any compile and link pair, where the
  pattern is never confirmed. It is also open whether the is_command_b
  call made for every token on every line is a performance problem on
  large input files.

  Functions involved:
    input_word_is_command
    input_find_command_start
    input_build_prefix_pattern
    input_match_prefix_pattern
    input_line_classify
    input_detect_prefix_pattern
    input_process_file

- genmakefile-multitarget-o-must-be-in-first-segment:
  The multi target syntax is "genmakefile [common-options] [--]
  [target1-opts] -- [target2-opts] ...". Each segment is parsed by
  parse_target_options, called from main in genmakefile.c. The option -o
  or --output only takes effect in the first segment, either the common
  options or target1.

  The reason is that main calls open_output_file once, right after the
  first segment and before the loop over later segments. An -o in the
  second or a later segment sets the outfile global too late and the
  Makefile silently goes to stdout.

  Either document the restriction in --help, which currently lists -o under
  Options without mentioning it, or move the open_output_file call to after
  all segments have been parsed.

- path-lib-follow-up:
  The view based functions in lib/path have no man pages yet. The old pages
  of the removed families were deleted along with them. The list iterator
  path_listlen with PATH_LIST_FOREACH has no client of its own in c-utils,
  only path_search uses it.
  Plan and status are in ../shish/doc/path-lib.md section 5.9.

  Functions without man page:
    path_rootlen
    path_basename
    path_dirlen
    path_cat
    path_canon
    path_abs
    path_rel
    path_common
    path_extoff
    path_cat_sa
    path_canon_sa
    path_abs_sa
    path_rel_sa

  Removed families whose pages are gone:
    path_concat
    path_collapse
    path_canonical
    path_normalize
    path_absolute
    path_relative_to

- path-lib-adopt-remaining:
  A survey of 2026-10-17 found much hand rolled path handling that does not
  use lib/path.h yet. The functions path_isname, path_isrel, path_parentlen,
  path_common and path_rootlen also have no caller outside lib/path and the
  tests, so adopting them in the places below would exercise them.

  A parallel path family that should be replaced by the library:
    src/genmakefile/path.c with path_prefix, path_extension, path_output,
      path_wildcard, path_clean, path_normalize_sa, path_dirname_alloc,
      add_path
    about 40 callers in genmakefile.c and src/genmakefile
    lib/str/str_basename.c, about 60 callers, replace by path_basename_s
    lib/unix/fnmatch.c, fnmatch_b.c and glob.c, compare path_fnmatch
    lib/unix/symlink.c, Windows drive and UNC tests, GetFullPathName

